  <h2>What a deload is</h2>
  <p>A group of strength and physique coaches reached a shared definition in 2023: a deload is a period of reduced training stress meant to ease physical and mental fatigue, help you recover, and leave you better prepared for the next block of training. Those same authors describe deloading as widely used and under-researched. That matters for everything below. Nobody has run the trial that says how long a deload should be or how far to cut.</p>

  <h2>Jordan's rule: half the volume, same weights</h2>
  <p>In his coaching interview, Jordan was asked how he deloads. His answer: drop the volume, keep the intensity generally the same, and cut volume in half.</p>
  <p>For the bench press, that means the bar stays about as heavy as it was and you do half as many sets. If your normal day is 4 sets of 5 at 220 lb, the deload day is 2 sets of 5 at 220 lb. You still train on your usual days, so the rhythm of the week does not change.</p>

  <h2>What 246 competitive lifters do</h2>
  <p>Rogerson and colleagues surveyed 246 competitive strength and physique athletes in 2024 (181 men and 65 women, average age 29.5, with about 8 years of training). It is a survey, not a trial, and the sample was people who chose to respond. With that said, here is what they reported:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>All of them deloaded.</li>
    <li>A typical deload lasted about 6 days (6.4 on average), and came about every 5 to 6 weeks (5.6 on average), with a wide spread.</li>
    <li>They planned deloads in advance, or combined a plan with adjusting on the fly.</li>
    <li>They also deloaded when performance stalled, or when muscles or joints felt sore or achy.</li>
    <li>They cut volume by doing fewer reps per set and fewer sets per week, but kept training the same number of days.</li>
    <li>They lowered the load and reduced effort by leaving more reps in reserve.</li>
    <li>They generally kept the same exercises.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The one place this differs from Jordan's rule is the load. The surveyed athletes generally lowered it. Jordan keeps it. Both cut volume, and both keep you training. If you feel only mildly tired, the same weights with half the sets is a light touch. If you are beaten up, lowering the weight as well is the more cautious version.</p>

  <h2>How to tell you need one</h2>
  <p>Jordan's signs are the ones lifters notice first:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Your sleep is poor.</li>
    <li>Training feels like a grind.</li>
    <li>You feel worn out.</li>
    <li>Your numbers are trending down, or have not gone up in a very long time.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The survey adds two triggers: a stall in performance, and unusual soreness in the muscles or joints. Jordan notes that in a maintenance phase or an in-season phase you are not trying to get stronger, only to recover from game to game, so those signs mean something different there.</p>

  <h2>A sample deload week for the bench</h2>
  <p>These numbers are an example to show the idea, not a prescription. The lighter-load version uses 5 to 10 percent off as an illustration. The survey did not specify an amount.</p>
  <div class="chart-wrap">
    <table class="pct-table data-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Day</th><th>Normal week</th><th>Jordan's version</th><th>Lighter version</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Heavy day</td><td>4 x 5 at 220 lb</td><td>2 x 5 at 220 lb</td><td>2 x 5 at 205 lb</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Volume day</td><td>4 x 8 at 185 lb</td><td>2 x 8 at 185 lb</td><td>2 x 8 at 170 lb</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Technique day</td><td>3 x 5, paused, at 175 lb</td><td>Skip a set: 2 x 5 at 175 lb</td><td>2 x 5 at 160 lb</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>Keep the bar speed crisp and stop each set with plenty in reserve. The point is to feel better at the end of the week than at the start.</p>

  <h2>What to do after</h2>
  <p>Go back to your normal volume and weights. If your numbers come back up, the deload did its job. If they do not, Jordan's list for a lift that stops going up is overtraining, under-recovery, or an injury you may not know about. He says being at your genetic limit is possible but rare outside professional lifters. Rule those out, then look at <a href="/why-my-bench-press-is-weak/">technique and programming</a>.</p>
